\section{Helper Functions}

We need some utility functions for formatting, display, and autocompletion.

\subsection{Label Display Constants}

When displaying labels in different contexts, we use consistent separators.
For CSV export (where labels appear in a single cell), we use a hierarchical
separator that clearly distinguishes label boundaries while remaining
human-readable.

<<constants>>=
# Label display separators
LABEL_SEPARATOR = " > "

# Default time limits for work-life balance warnings
DEFAULT_WEEKLY_LIMIT_HOURS = 40.0  # Standard full-time work week
DEFAULT_DAILY_LIMIT_HOURS = 8.0    # Standard work day
@

Durations are normally positive, but they must never be \emph{rendered}
as positive when they are not.  Python's [[divmod]] floors towards
negative infinity, so splitting a raw negative second count into hours,
minutes and seconds yields components that read as a plausible positive
duration: $-30$~minutes becomes [[30m 0s]], and so does $-90$~minutes.
A corrupt entry would then be indistinguishable from a healthy one in
[[status]], [[ls]], and [[stats]] output.

We therefore split the magnitude and carry the sign as an explicit
prefix.  This is a display safeguard rather than a licence to store
negative durations---the commands that build entries reject inverted
intervals---but a formatter that cannot lie is what makes such a bug
visible instead of silent.

<<helper functions>>=
def format_duration(duration: datetime.timedelta) -> str:
    """Format a duration in a human-readable way.

    Negative durations keep a leading minus sign so an
    inverted interval can never be displayed as a
    positive one.
    """
    total_seconds = int(duration.total_seconds())
    sign = "-" if total_seconds < 0 else ""
    hours, remainder = divmod(abs(total_seconds), 3600)
    minutes, seconds = divmod(remainder, 60)

    if hours > 0:
        return f"{sign}{hours}h {minutes}m {seconds}s"
    elif minutes > 0:
        return f"{sign}{minutes}m {seconds}s"
    else:
        return f"{sign}{seconds}s"

def get_labels_display(labels: list[str]) -> str:
    """Get a display string for labels (as comma-separated tags)"""
    return ", ".join(sorted(labels)) if labels else "No labels"
@

<<test functions>>=
def test_format_duration_is_sign_aware():
  """Negative durations must not render as positive ones."""
  assert format_duration(datetime.timedelta(minutes=30)) == "30m 0s"
  assert format_duration(datetime.timedelta(minutes=-30)) == "-30m 0s"
  assert format_duration(datetime.timedelta(minutes=-90)) == "-1h 30m 0s"
  assert format_duration(datetime.timedelta(seconds=-5)) == "-5s"
  assert format_duration(datetime.timedelta()) == "0s"
@

\subsubsection{Abbreviating Content Hashes}

The [[track ls --id]] view names each entry by a Git-style prefix of its
SHA256 [[content_hash()]].  We want the shortest prefix that still uniquely
identifies every displayed entry, so we start at a minimum length and widen
only when two prefixes collide.

The minimum matters more than it first appears.  A pure
shortest-unique-prefix rule would collapse to a \emph{single character}
whenever the first hex digits already differ---which is the common case with
only a handful of entries, and especially right after an edit changes the set
of hashes.  A one-character id is unreadable and feels unstable: it changes
length as entries come and go.  We therefore floor the prefix at six
characters, matching Git's default abbreviation, which is long enough to read
and re-type while the collision loop below still widens further on the rare
occasions six is not enough.

<<constants>>=
TRACKING_HASH_PREFIX_MIN_LENGTH = 6
@

Because [[resolve_tracking_entry]] matches any prefix with [[startswith]],
widening the displayed id never invalidates a shorter prefix a user may have
already copied---display length and lookup length are independent.

<<helper functions>>=
def abbreviate_hashes(full_hashes: list[str]) -> dict[str, str]:
    """Return unique short prefixes for full content hashes."""
    if not full_hashes:
        return {}

    prefix_length = TRACKING_HASH_PREFIX_MIN_LENGTH
    while True:
        prefixes = {
            full_hash: full_hash[:prefix_length]
            for full_hash in full_hashes
        }
        if len(set(prefixes.values())) == len(full_hashes):
            return prefixes
        prefix_length += 1
@

\subsubsection{Merging Overlapping Intervals}

Naively summing [[entry.duration()]] across all entries double-counts
wall-clock time whenever labels overlap---for instance, two labels
tracked from 09:00 to 10:00 would report two hours instead of one.
We avoid this by merging overlapping intervals before summing.  The
algorithm sorts intervals by start time, then greedily extends the
current merged interval as long as the next one overlaps; whenever
there is a gap it flushes the accumulated interval and starts a new
one.  We expose the interval-merging step directly because the stats
command needs it in two places: once for overall day totals, and once
for exact label combinations within a day.

<<helper functions>>=
def merge_interval_pairs(intervals) -> datetime.timedelta:
    """Compute wall-clock time covered by ``(start, end)`` pairs."""
    intervals = sorted(intervals)
    if not intervals:
        return datetime.timedelta()

    merged_total = datetime.timedelta()
    cur_start, cur_end = intervals[0]

    for start, end in intervals[1:]:
        if start <= cur_end:
            cur_end = max(cur_end, end)
        else:
            merged_total += cur_end - cur_start
            cur_start, cur_end = start, end

    merged_total += cur_end - cur_start
    return merged_total

def merge_intervals(
    entries: "list[TrackingEntry]",
) -> datetime.timedelta:
    """Compute wall-clock time covered by entries.

    Overlapping intervals are merged so that concurrent
    labels do not inflate the total.
    """
    return merge_interval_pairs(
        (e.start_time, e.end_time) for e in entries
    )
@

\subsubsection{Daily Aggregation}

The stats report works per calendar date.  An entry that spans midnight
must be split across the two dates; otherwise all its time would land on
the start date.  We therefore split each entry at midnight boundaries
first.

There are two useful views of the split data:
\begin{description}
\item[Daily wall-clock totals] Merge all intervals within a day so
  overlapping labels do not inflate the day's total.
\item[Daily label-set totals] Keep exact label combinations together so
  repeated sessions with the same labels collapse into one line, while
  still merging overlaps within that label set.
\end{description}

We also generate the requested date range explicitly so that days off
remain visible as [[0s]] rather than disappearing from the report.

<<helper functions>>=
def compute_daily_wall_clock(
    entries: "list[TrackingEntry]",
) -> dict:
    """Compute wall-clock time per calendar date.

    Returns a dict mapping ``datetime.date`` to
    ``datetime.timedelta``, with overlapping intervals
    merged within each date.
    """
    date_intervals = {}
    for entry in entries:
        start = entry.start_time
        end = entry.end_time
        while start.date() < end.date():
            midnight = datetime.datetime.combine(
                start.date() + datetime.timedelta(days=1),
                datetime.time.min,
            )
            date_intervals.setdefault(
                start.date(), []
            ).append((start, midnight))
            start = midnight
        if start < end:
            date_intervals.setdefault(
                start.date(), []
            ).append((start, end))

    daily_totals = {}
    for date, intervals in date_intervals.items():
        daily_totals[date] = merge_interval_pairs(intervals)

    return daily_totals

def compute_daily_label_set_totals(
    entries: "list[TrackingEntry]",
) -> dict[datetime.date, dict[tuple, datetime.timedelta]]:
    """Compute merged time per date and exact label set."""
    date_label_intervals = {}
    for entry in entries:
        labels_key = tuple(sorted(entry.labels))
        start = entry.start_time
        end = entry.end_time
        while start.date() < end.date():
            midnight = datetime.datetime.combine(
                start.date() + datetime.timedelta(days=1),
                datetime.time.min,
            )
            date_label_intervals.setdefault(
                start.date(), {}
            ).setdefault(labels_key, []).append((start, midnight))
            start = midnight
        if start < end:
            date_label_intervals.setdefault(
                start.date(), {}
            ).setdefault(labels_key, []).append((start, end))

    daily_totals = {}
    for date, label_intervals in date_label_intervals.items():
        daily_totals[date] = {
            labels_key: merge_interval_pairs(intervals)
            for labels_key, intervals in label_intervals.items()
        }

    return daily_totals

def make_stats_dates(
    days: int,
    now: datetime.datetime | None = None,
) -> list[datetime.date]:
    """Return the reporting dates, newest first."""
    if now is None:
        now = datetime.datetime.now()

    return [
        now.date() - datetime.timedelta(days=offset)
        for offset in range(days)
    ]

def filter_entries_for_stats_window(
    entries: "list[TrackingEntry]",
    days: int,
    now: datetime.datetime | None = None,
) -> "list[TrackingEntry]":
    """Return entries clipped to the stats calendar window.

    The stats command reports whole calendar days, not a rolling
    ``24 * days`` hour window. An overnight entry that starts before
    midnight but continues into today must therefore remain visible,
    while late work from yesterday should not leak into today's
    summary just because it happened within the last 24 hours.
    """
    if now is None:
        now = datetime.datetime.now()

    start_date = now.date() - datetime.timedelta(days=days - 1)
    window_start = datetime.datetime.combine(
        start_date,
        datetime.time.min,
    )
    window_end = datetime.datetime.combine(
        now.date() + datetime.timedelta(days=1),
        datetime.time.min,
    )

    filtered = []
    for entry in entries:
        if entry.end_time <= window_start:
            continue
        if entry.start_time >= window_end:
            continue

        filtered.append(
            TrackingEntry(
                start_time=max(entry.start_time, window_start),
                end_time=min(entry.end_time, window_end),
                labels=entry.labels,
                description=entry.description,
                who=entry.who,
            )
        )

    return filtered
@

\subsubsection{Shell Completion Helpers}

Shell-completion functions help the user discover available labels and
worker names interactively.  They silently return an empty list on any
error so that a broken session file never prevents tab completion:

<<helper functions>>=
def complete_active_labels(ctx, args, incomplete):
    """
    Shell completion function for active labels (used by stop command).

    Returns currently tracking labels that match the incomplete
    string.
    """
    try:
        session = load_active_session()
        active = session.get_active_labels()
        return [
            label for label in active
            if label.startswith(incomplete)
        ]
    except:
        return []

def complete_historical_labels(ctx, args, incomplete):
    """
    Shell completion function for historical labels
    (used by start command).

    Returns previously used labels from tracking history
    that match the incomplete string.
    """
    try:
        entries = load_tracking_data()
        # Collect all unique labels from historical entries
        all_labels = set()
        for entry in entries:
            all_labels.update(entry.labels)

        # Also include currently active labels
        session = load_active_session()
        all_labels.update(session.get_active_labels())

        return sorted([
            label for label in all_labels
            if label.startswith(incomplete)
        ])
    except:
        return []

def complete_workers(ctx, args, incomplete):
    """
    Shell completion function for worker names.

    Discovers workers from active session files and
    historical tracking data.
    """
    try:
        workers = {default_username}
        tracking_dir = get_tracking_dir()

        # Find active workers from session files
        if tracking_dir.exists():
            import glob as glob_mod
            for f in tracking_dir.glob(
                "current_session_*.json"
            ):
                name = f.stem.replace(
                    "current_session_", ""
                )
                if name:
                    workers.add(name)

        # Find historical workers from tracking data
        entries = load_tracking_data()
        for entry in entries:
            workers.add(entry.who)

        return sorted([
            w for w in workers
            if w.startswith(incomplete)
        ])
    except:
        return []

def parse_offset(offset_str: str) -> datetime.timedelta:
    """Parse an offset string into a timedelta.

    Bare number = minutes.  Suffixes: ``s``,
    ``m``, ``h``, ``d``.  Negative values
    (``-30m``, ``-1h30m``) and composite values
    (``1h30m``) are accepted.
    """
    minutes = _parse_duration(
        offset_str.strip(),
        {"s": 1 / 60, "m": 1,
         "h": 60, "d": 24 * 60},
    )
    return datetime.timedelta(minutes=minutes)

def parse_at_time(at_str: str, reference_time: datetime.datetime = None) -> datetime.datetime:
    """
    Parse an absolute time string into a datetime.
    
    Supports formats:
    - "HH:MM" - time today in 24-hour format
    - "YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM" - specific date and time
    
    Examples:
        parse_at_time("14:30") -> today at 14:30
        parse_at_time("2024-12-13 14:30") -> Dec 13, 2024 at 14:30
    
    Args:
        at_str: The time string to parse
        reference_time: The reference time for relative parsing (default: now)
    
    Returns:
        datetime object representing the specified time
    """
    if reference_time is None:
        reference_time = datetime.datetime.now()
    
    at_str = at_str.strip()
    
    # Try parsing "YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM" format
    try:
        return datetime.datetime.strptime(at_str, "%Y-%m-%d %H:%M")
    except ValueError:
        pass
    
    # Try parsing "HH:MM" format (time today)
    try:
        time_obj = datetime.datetime.strptime(at_str, "%H:%M").time()
        return datetime.datetime.combine(reference_time.date(), time_obj)
    except ValueError:
        pass
    
    raise ValueError(
        f"Invalid time format: '{at_str}'. "
        f"Use 'HH:MM' (24-hour) or 'YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM'"
    )
@

\subsection{Testing Helper Functions}

<<test functions>>=
def test_parse_offset():
    """Test offset parsing with various formats"""
    # Plain number (minutes)
    assert parse_offset("30") == datetime.timedelta(minutes=30)
    assert parse_offset("-30") == datetime.timedelta(minutes=-30)
    
    # Minutes with suffix
    assert parse_offset("30m") == datetime.timedelta(minutes=30)
    assert parse_offset("-30m") == datetime.timedelta(minutes=-30)
    
    # Hours with suffix
    assert parse_offset("1h") == datetime.timedelta(hours=1)
    assert parse_offset("1.5h") == datetime.timedelta(hours=1.5)
    assert parse_offset("-2h") == datetime.timedelta(hours=-2)
    assert parse_offset("-0.5h") == datetime.timedelta(minutes=-30)

    # Composite
    assert parse_offset("1h30m") == \
        datetime.timedelta(minutes=90)
    assert parse_offset("-1h30m") == \
        datetime.timedelta(minutes=-90)

    # New units now also accepted
    assert parse_offset("90s") == \
        datetime.timedelta(seconds=90)

def test_parse_at_time():
    """Test absolute time parsing"""
    # Test HH:MM format
    ref_time = datetime.datetime(2024, 12, 13, 10, 0, 0)
    result = parse_at_time("14:30", ref_time)
    assert result.hour == 14
    assert result.minute == 30
    assert result.date() == ref_time.date()
    
    # Test YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM format
    result = parse_at_time("2024-12-25 16:45")
    assert result.year == 2024
    assert result.month == 12
    assert result.day == 25
    assert result.hour == 16
    assert result.minute == 45
    
    # Test invalid format
    try:
        parse_at_time("invalid")
        assert False, "Should have raised ValueError"
    except ValueError as e:
        assert "Invalid time format" in str(e)
