\section{Subcommands}

Now we implement the various subcommands for the track functionality. Each command
is organized with its documentation, implementation, and tests together.

\subsection{Start Command}

The start command begins tracking time with the specified labels. Labels are
independent tags that can be started individually or in groups.

For work that is tied to a subprocess, [[start]] can also launch a command.
The canonical form now uses a literal [[--]] separator so the child receives
an exact argv list:

\begin{verbatim}
nytid track start [LABELS...] [OPTIONS] -- PROG [ARG ...]
\end{verbatim}

This matters most for wrapper scripts.  A shell wrapper can forward
[["$@"]] losslessly, but it cannot flatten the same argv back into a single
string without destroying word boundaries.  The legacy [[--command]]/[[-c]]
string form remains available for existing callers, but new wrappers and
manual invocations should prefer the [[-- prog args]] form.  As before,
[[--pane]] and [[--window]] open a tracked shell in tmux, and launch-bound
labels stop automatically when the subprocess exits.

We use noweb's chunk concatenation to build up the command's options 
incrementally. The [[<<start command options>>]] chunk accumulates function 
parameters as we introduce each feature, while [[<<start command 
implementation>>]] contains the core logic.

<<argument and option definitions>>=
labels_arg = typer.Argument(
    help="Labels to track. "
         "If empty, resumes last stopped labels.",
    autocompletion=complete_historical_labels)
@

<<start command>>=
