We load the active session first only for read-only preflight decisions
such as resume and launch validation.  The actual state mutation happens
 later through [[start_tracking_labels()]], which reloads the worker's
 current session under lock before writing anything.  That keeps long-
 running composed commands from relying on stale in-memory session state.

<<load session and resolve labels>>=
session = load_active_session(who)

# If no labels provided, try to resume last stopped labels
if not labels:
    if command_args is not None:
        labels = []
    elif session.last_stopped:
        labels = session.last_stopped
        typer.echo(
            f"Resuming: {', '.join(sorted(labels))}"
        )
    else:
        typer.echo(
            "Error: No labels specified and no "
            "previous labels to resume", err=True
        )
        typer.echo(
            "Usage: nytid track start "
            "<label1> [label2] ...", err=True
        )
        raise typer.Exit(1)
@

After resolving labels, we start tracking and report what happened.
Any [[--replace]] handling is delegated to [[start_tracking_labels()]] so
the discard and restart happen against fresh on-disk state rather than
against this earlier snapshot.

When [[start]] launches a command or tmux shell, it may need to derive one
extra label from the command name and validate tmux usage.  When no
command was named, [[build_launch_shell]] picks the shell---plain tmux
or prompted direct---and seeds the prompt context as part of building
it.  An explicit command builds no shell, so there we seed the context
into its environment ourselves; it is harmless for a non-shell command
and lets an explicitly launched shell still show the marker.

Launch mode also needs a clear ownership rule for labels.  If a label is
already active and we launch a child process without saying what should
happen to the existing session, the command would appear to belong to that
label but the automatic shutdown path could leave the old session running.
We therefore require launch-bound labels to be inactive unless the user
explicitly picks one of two escape hatches: [[--replace]] (discard the old
session and restart) or [[--continue]] (keep the old session and bind the
child to it).  The shutdown path treats both cases identically---one
[[TrackingEntry]] per label at child exit---so the only difference is
whether the original start time survives.

The same rule must also cover the implicit label contributed by
[[--command]].  Otherwise a launch could smuggle in an already-active
command label after the preflight check and silently downgrade a conflict
to [[Already tracking]].

The effective launch label set should also be the one we report back to the
user.  If an explicit label matches the implicit command basename, keeping
the raw pre-deduplicated list would produce noisy output such as
[[Already tracking: echo, echo]] even though the session state correctly
tracks only one [[echo]] label.

Derived tmux work sometimes needs one more label source: the current
tracked context.  [[--inherit-labels]] reads labels from the current
tracked tmux window, or from the current tracked pane when no tracked
window owns that pane.  This is intentionally narrower than the worker's
full active session, so unrelated labels active elsewhere do not leak
into the derived launch.

Those inherited labels are already owned by the current tracked context,
so launch mode may borrow them without an explicit [[--continue]].  The
same exemption must not apply to unrelated implicit command labels,
which still need [[--replace]] unless the inherited context already
contains them.

Concretely: an implicit command label is exempted from [[--replace]]
only when it is part of the inherited context, not when it merely
matches a [[--continue]]-borrowed explicit label.  This is why the
conflict check for command labels filters on
[[inherited_context_labels]] directly rather than on the broader
[[borrowable_launch_labels]] set, while the explicit-label check uses
[[borrowable_launch_labels]] so [[--continue]] keeps working as before.

Track and todo should also present launch context the same way.  We therefore
compute one context string up front and reuse it for tmux window names and
as a best-effort [[PS1]] seed.  In tmux the user's [[~/.bashrc]] will
likely overwrite [[PS1]] during normal startup, so the tmux window name is
the primary context indicator for pane and window launches.

<<prepare launch mode>>=
launch_requested = (
    command_args is not None or pane or window
    or bool(wd_path) or bool(worktree)
)
tracking_labels = list(labels)
explicit_launch_labels = list(tracking_labels)
inherited_context_labels = []
borrowable_launch_labels = []
launch_owned_labels = list(tracking_labels)
launch_cleanup_labels = list(tracking_labels)
proc_env = None
proc_cwd = os.getcwd()
worktree_dir = None
prompt_file = None
prompt_dir = None
launch_context = None
window_name = None

if block is None:
    block = not (pane or window)

if not block and not (pane or window):
    typer.echo(
        "Error: --no-block requires "
        "--pane or --window",
        err=True,
    )
    raise typer.Exit(1)

if timeout is not None and command_args is None:
    typer.echo(
        "Error: --timeout requires --command",
        err=True,
    )
    raise typer.Exit(1)

if launch_requested:
    try:
        validate_tmux_options(pane, window)
    except ValueError as exc:
        typer.echo(str(exc), err=True)
        raise typer.Exit(1)

    use_auto_suspend = should_auto_suspend(
        who, auto_suspend,
    )
    if use_auto_suspend and pane:
        warn_if_focus_events_off()

    <<resolve working directory>>
    proc_cwd = wd_path if wd_path else proc_cwd
    <<fork the launch into a worktree>>
