\subsubsection{Git Worktrees}
\label{track:worktrees}

The [[--wd]] option decides \emph{where} a launched command runs.  When
that directory is a git repository, the natural next question is
\emph{on what checkout}: an agent asked to fix a bug, or a grading
session that edits a lab skeleton, should not scribble on the branch the
teacher has checked out in the main working tree.  Git worktrees give
each such session its own checkout of the same repository, so the
[[--worktree]] option complements [[--wd]]: the base directory is still
[[--wd]] (or the current directory when [[--wd]] is absent), but the
launch is forked off into a worktree of the repository containing that
base directory.

We mirror the layout of [[claude --worktree]] exactly---the worktree
lives at [[<repo>/.claude/worktrees/<name>]] on a branch named
[[worktree-<name>]]---rather than inventing a nytid-specific one.  The
two tools then interoperate: a todo whose command is [[claude]] lands in
a checkout that Claude Code recognises as one of its own worktrees, and
a worktree left behind by either tool is picked up by the other.  The
repository root comes from [[git rev-parse --show-toplevel]] of the base
directory, not from [[--git-common-dir]]: if the base directory is
itself a worktree we nest under it, which is what a user who deliberately
launched from a worktree would expect.

<<argument and option definitions>>=
worktree_opt = typer.Option(
    "--worktree",
    help="Run in git worktree NAME "
         "(<repo>/.claude/worktrees/NAME on "
         "branch worktree-NAME) of the repo "
         "containing --wd or the current "
         "directory; removed on exit if "
         "left unchanged",
)
@

Only the long form exists: [[-w]] already means [[--who]] and [[-W]]
means [[--window]] on the same commands.

Every git question below is a subprocess call.  We keep the wrapper
minimal---capture output, never raise---and let each caller decide what
a failure means, because the same command (\enquote{is this a
repository?}) is an error at launch time but merely \enquote{nothing to
clean up} at exit time.  A missing [[git]] binary is treated like any
other failure so the callers need only one branch.

<<helper functions>>=
def run_git(*args, cwd=None):
    """Run `git ARGS` in `cwd`; return the CompletedProcess or None.

    Returns None when git cannot be executed at all.  A non-zero
    exit is returned as-is so callers can inspect stderr.
    """
    try:
        return subprocess.run(
            ["git", *args],
            cwd=cwd,
            capture_output=True,
            text=True,
        )
    except OSError:
        return None
@

The worktree name is a user-supplied string that we join onto a path
and onto a branch name, so it must not contain path separators (which
would escape [[.claude/worktrees/]]) and it must not be empty.  We
validate it once here and every entry point---[[track start]], and the
[[todo]] commands that persist the name---calls this instead of
re-checking.

<<helper functions>>=
def validate_worktree_name(name):
    """Return `name` if it is a usable worktree name.

    Raises ValueError for empty names or names containing a path
    separator, which would escape the worktrees directory.
    """
    if not name or not name.strip():
        raise ValueError("worktree name must not be empty")
    if os.sep in name or (os.altsep and os.altsep in name):
        raise ValueError(
            f"worktree name must not contain "
            f"path separators: {name!r}"
        )
    return name.strip()
@

The path and branch conventions live in one function so that creation
and cleanup cannot drift apart.

<<constants>>=
WORKTREES_SUBDIR = os.path.join(".claude", "worktrees")
WORKTREE_BRANCH_PREFIX = "worktree-"
<<helper functions>>=
def worktree_location(toplevel, name):
    """Return `(worktree_dir, branch)` for `name` under `toplevel`."""
    return (
        os.path.join(toplevel, WORKTREES_SUBDIR, name),
        f"{WORKTREE_BRANCH_PREFIX}{name}",
    )
@

Creating the worktree has to be idempotent, because [[todo start]]
runs it every time a todo with a stored worktree is (re)started, and
because a previous session may have kept the worktree (it had changes)
or removed the directory but kept the branch (the user ran
[[git worktree remove]] by hand).  Reuse is the whole point: a task
that was interrupted must be restartable and resume exactly where it
left off, so an existing worktree is never recreated or reset.  We
therefore distinguish three states.  If the directory is already a
checkout whose toplevel is itself, we simply reuse it.  Otherwise, if
the branch exists we attach a new worktree to that branch, so the
user's earlier commits are what they land on; only when neither exists
do we create the branch from the repository's current [[HEAD]].  A
directory that exists but is not a worktree is an error rather than
something to overwrite: it is either the user's data or a broken
checkout, and both deserve a look.

The directory-gone case has a trap: git remembers every worktree it
added, and refuses to add one at a path it still has on record as
\enquote{missing but registered}.  A [[git worktree remove]] clears
that record, but an [[rm -rf]]---a tmp cleaner, an interrupted removal,
a user tidying by hand---does not.  We therefore run
[[git worktree prune]] before adding; it drops only records whose
directory is gone, so live worktrees are untouched, and it makes the
restart succeed instead of demanding that the user know about
[[prune]].

<<helper functions>>=
def ensure_worktree(base_dir, name):
    """Create or reuse worktree `name` for the repo containing `base_dir`.

    Returns the worktree directory.  Raises ValueError with a
    user-facing message when `base_dir` is not inside a git
    repository or git refuses to create the worktree.
    """
    name = validate_worktree_name(name)
    <<resolve the repository toplevel>>
    worktree_dir, branch = worktree_location(toplevel, name)
    <<reuse an existing worktree checkout>>
    <<attach or create the worktree branch>>
    return worktree_dir
@

<<resolve the repository toplevel>>=
result = run_git(
    "rev-parse", "--show-toplevel", cwd=base_dir,
)
if result is None or result.returncode != 0:
    raise ValueError(
        f"--worktree requires a git repository, "
        f"but {base_dir} is not inside one"
    )
toplevel = result.stdout.strip()
@

We compare real paths because git prints the toplevel with symlinks
resolved, while [[worktree_dir]] was built from whatever the user
typed for [[--wd]].

<<reuse an existing worktree checkout>>=
if os.path.isdir(worktree_dir):
    probe = run_git(
        "rev-parse", "--show-toplevel", cwd=worktree_dir,
    )
    if (
        probe is not None and probe.returncode == 0
        and os.path.realpath(probe.stdout.strip())
        == os.path.realpath(worktree_dir)
    ):
        return worktree_dir
    raise ValueError(
        f"{worktree_dir} exists but is not a git worktree"
    )
@

<<attach or create the worktree branch>>=
run_git("worktree", "prune", cwd=toplevel)
os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(worktree_dir), exist_ok=True)
branch_exists = run_git(
    "rev-parse", "--verify", "--quiet",
    f"refs/heads/{branch}", cwd=toplevel,
)
if branch_exists is not None and branch_exists.returncode == 0:
    add_args = ["worktree", "add", worktree_dir, branch]
else:
    add_args = ["worktree", "add", "-b", branch, worktree_dir]
added = run_git(*add_args, cwd=toplevel)
if added is None or added.returncode != 0:
    detail = (added.stderr.strip() if added else "git not found")
    raise ValueError(
        f"could not create worktree {worktree_dir}: {detail}"
    )
@

Let us verify the three creation states against a real repository.  Git
is a hard requirement of the feature, so the tests use it directly
rather than mocking; a scratch repository with one commit is enough.
The commits are made with an explicit identity and with signing turned
off, so the tests do not depend on the developer's global git
configuration.

<<test functions>>=
TEST_GIT_CONFIG = [
  "-c", "user.name=Test",
  "-c", "user.email=t@example.com",
  "-c", "commit.gpgsign=false",
]


def make_git_repo(path):
  """Initialise a git repository with one commit at `path`."""
  path = str(path)
  os.makedirs(path, exist_ok=True)
  subprocess.run(["git", "init", "-q", path], check=True)
  git = ["git", "-C", path] + TEST_GIT_CONFIG
  with open(os.path.join(path, "README"), "w") as f:
    f.write("hello\n")
  subprocess.run(git + ["add", "README"], check=True)
  subprocess.run(git + ["commit", "-q", "-m", "init"], check=True)
  return path


def test_ensure_worktree_creates_dir_and_branch(tmp_path):
  """A fresh name creates .claude/worktrees/NAME on worktree-NAME."""
  repo = make_git_repo(tmp_path / "repo")
  wt = ensure_worktree(repo, "feat")
  assert wt == os.path.join(repo, ".claude", "worktrees", "feat")
  assert os.path.isdir(wt)
  branch = subprocess.run(
    ["git", "-C", wt, "rev-parse", "--abbrev-ref", "HEAD"],
    capture_output=True, text=True, check=True,
  ).stdout.strip()
  assert branch == "worktree-feat"


def test_ensure_worktree_reuses_existing(tmp_path):
  """Calling twice returns the same worktree without error."""
  repo = make_git_repo(tmp_path / "repo")
  first = ensure_worktree(repo, "feat")
  second = ensure_worktree(repo, "feat")
  assert first == second


def test_ensure_worktree_reattaches_existing_branch(tmp_path):
  """A surviving branch is reused when the directory is gone."""
  repo = make_git_repo(tmp_path / "repo")
  wt = ensure_worktree(repo, "feat")
  subprocess.run(
    ["git", "-C", repo, "worktree", "remove", wt], check=True,
  )
  assert not os.path.isdir(wt)
  wt2 = ensure_worktree(repo, "feat")
  assert wt2 == wt and os.path.isdir(wt2)
  branches = subprocess.run(
    ["git", "-C", repo, "branch", "--list", "worktree-feat"],
    capture_output=True, text=True, check=True,
  ).stdout
  assert branches.count("worktree-feat") == 1


