@

\paragraph{Where in the worktree.}
[[--wd]] may point below the repository root, and without
[[--worktree]] the launch would run exactly there.  Dropping the user
at the worktree root instead would make [[--worktree]] change the
working directory in a way that has nothing to do with worktrees, so
we carry the relative path over.  If that subdirectory does not exist
in the checkout (the branch predates it), the worktree root is the
only sensible fallback.

<<helper functions>>=
def worktree_launch_dir(base_dir, worktree_dir):
    """Return the directory inside `worktree_dir` matching `base_dir`.

    `base_dir` is where the launch would have run without a
    worktree; its path relative to the repository toplevel is
    reapplied inside the worktree, falling back to the worktree
    root when that subdirectory does not exist there.
    """
    toplevel = run_git(
        "rev-parse", "--show-toplevel", cwd=base_dir,
    )
    if toplevel is None or toplevel.returncode != 0:
        return worktree_dir
    rel = os.path.relpath(
        os.path.realpath(base_dir),
        os.path.realpath(toplevel.stdout.strip()),
    )
    if rel == os.curdir or rel.startswith(os.pardir):
        return worktree_dir
    candidate = os.path.join(worktree_dir, rel)
    if os.path.isdir(candidate):
        return candidate
    return worktree_dir
@

<<test functions>>=
def test_worktree_launch_dir_keeps_subdirectory(tmp_path):
  """A --wd below the toplevel maps to the same place in the worktree."""
  repo = make_git_repo(tmp_path / "repo")
  sub = os.path.join(repo, "src", "deep")
  os.makedirs(sub)
  git = ["git", "-C", repo] + TEST_GIT_CONFIG
  with open(os.path.join(sub, "f"), "w") as f:
    f.write("x\n")
  subprocess.run(git + ["add", "src"], check=True)
  subprocess.run(git + ["commit", "-q", "-m", "sub"], check=True)
  wt = ensure_worktree(sub, "feat")
  assert worktree_launch_dir(sub, wt) == os.path.join(
    wt, "src", "deep",
  )
  assert worktree_launch_dir(repo, wt) == wt


def test_worktree_launch_dir_falls_back_to_root(tmp_path):
  """A subdirectory absent from the checkout maps to the root."""
  repo = make_git_repo(tmp_path / "repo")
  sub = os.path.join(repo, "untracked-dir")
  os.makedirs(sub)
  wt = ensure_worktree(sub, "feat")
  assert worktree_launch_dir(sub, wt) == wt
@

\paragraph{Cleaning up.}
A worktree that the session never touched is clutter: an untracked
directory in the main checkout and a branch that points at the same
commit as where it started.  We remove it automatically---but only
then.  \enquote{Unchanged} means two things at once: the working tree
is clean ([[git status --porcelain]] prints nothing---untracked
\emph{and ignored} files included, since [[git worktree remove]] would
delete an ignored [[.env]] or agent scratch file without a word), and
the branch has no commits of its own, i.e.\ every commit reachable
from it is also reachable from some other branch.  Either
condition alone is not enough: a clean tree may sit on top of committed
work, and an unchanged branch may carry uncommitted edits.  When both
hold, removing the worktree and deleting the branch loses nothing that
[[HEAD]] of some other branch does not still have.

We ask git for the commits reachable from our branch but from no other
branch ([[--not --branches]] with our own branch excluded; the
[[--exclude]] glob is matched against the short name, without
[[refs/heads/]]) rather than comparing against a recorded base commit:
nothing has to be persisted between launch and cleanup, which matters
for the detached tmux path where cleanup runs in a fresh process.

<<helper functions>>=
def worktree_is_unchanged(worktree_dir):
    """Return whether `worktree_dir` is clean and has no own commits."""
    status = run_git(
        "status", "--porcelain", "--ignored=traditional",
        cwd=worktree_dir,
    )
    if status is None or status.returncode != 0:
        return False
    if status.stdout.strip():
        return False
    branch = run_git(
        "rev-parse", "--abbrev-ref", "HEAD", cwd=worktree_dir,
    )
    if branch is None or branch.returncode != 0:
        return False
    branch = branch.stdout.strip()
    own = run_git(
        "rev-list", "--count", branch,
        "--not", f"--exclude={branch}", "--branches",
        cwd=worktree_dir,
    )
    if own is None or own.returncode != 0:
        return False
    return own.stdout.strip() == "0"
@

Cleanup must never disturb the tracking bookkeeping that follows it,
so this helper swallows every failure and merely reports whether the
worktree went away.  The [[git worktree remove]] must run from the
main checkout, not from inside the directory being removed, so we
locate the main repository through [[--git-common-dir]] (made absolute
against the worktree, since git may print it relative to its cwd).  A
worktree that has already vanished (the user removed it during the
session) counts as nothing to do.  Branch deletion uses [[-D]] because
our own check has already established the branch is fully merged;
git's [[-d]] would compare against the current [[HEAD]] only and refuse
in perfectly safe situations.

<<helper functions>>=
def remove_worktree_if_unchanged(worktree_dir):
    """Remove `worktree_dir` and its branch if nothing changed.

    Returns True if the worktree was removed.  Never raises;
    a worktree with changes, or one that is already gone, is
    left alone and False is returned.
    """
    if not os.path.isdir(worktree_dir):
        return False
    if not worktree_is_unchanged(worktree_dir):
        return False
    branch = run_git(
        "rev-parse", "--abbrev-ref", "HEAD", cwd=worktree_dir,
    )
    toplevel = run_git(
        "rev-parse", "--git-common-dir", cwd=worktree_dir,
    )
    if (
        branch is None or branch.returncode != 0
        or toplevel is None or toplevel.returncode != 0
    ):
        return False
    branch = branch.stdout.strip()
    common = toplevel.stdout.strip()
    if not os.path.isabs(common):
        common = os.path.join(worktree_dir, common)
    common = os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(common))
    removed = run_git(
        "worktree", "remove", worktree_dir, cwd=common,
    )
    if removed is None or removed.returncode != 0:
        return False
    run_git("branch", "-D", branch, cwd=common)
    return True
@

Both [[track start]] and [[todo start]] end a worktree launch by
calling this and telling the user what happened.  The wording matters,
because there are three different situations and the user's next move
differs: a removed worktree must not be looked for, a kept one must be
returned to, and one that could not be examined (already gone, or git
failed) is worth a glance.  We keep the message in one helper so both
commands say the same thing.

<<helper functions>>=
def report_worktree_cleanup(worktree_dir):
    """Remove `worktree_dir` if unchanged and describe the outcome."""
    if not os.path.isdir(worktree_dir):
        typer.echo(
            f"Worktree {worktree_dir} is already gone"
        )
        return
    if remove_worktree_if_unchanged(worktree_dir):
        typer.echo(
            f"Removed unchanged worktree {worktree_dir}"
        )
    elif worktree_is_unchanged(worktree_dir):
        typer.echo(
            f"Could not remove worktree {worktree_dir}",
            err=True,
        )
    else:
        typer.echo(
            f"Kept worktree {worktree_dir} (has changes)"
        )
@

<<test functions>>=
def test_report_worktree_cleanup_messages(tmp_path):
