Several options ([[--timeout]], [[--duration]], [[--estimate]]) accept
duration strings with an optional unit suffix: [[s]] (seconds), [[m]]
(minutes), [[h]] (hours), [[d]] (days).  Fractional values such as
[[1.5h]] (90~minutes) are accepted.  Composite durations such as
[[3h15m]] (3~hours plus 15~minutes) are accepted too; the parts are
summed.  The only difference between the options is what a bare number
(no suffix) means: seconds for [[--timeout]], hours for [[--duration]]
and [[--estimate]].

We factor this into a shared core that maps suffixes to multipliers.  It
first accepts a bare number directly, then tokenises concatenated
number-unit pairs and requires them to rebuild the input, which rejects
leftovers such as [[3h15]].  Thin wrappers each supply the right table:

<<constants>>=
_DURATION_TOKEN = re.compile(
    r"(\d+\.?\d*|\.\d+)([a-zA-Z]?)"
)
@

<<helper functions>>=
def _parse_duration(value, suffixes):
    """Apply a suffix multiplier table to a
    duration string.

    Either the whole string is a bare number
    (interpreted in the wrapper's default unit),
    or it is a sequence of ``<number><unit>``
    pairs that are summed (e.g. ``3h15m``).
    An optional leading ``+``/``-`` sign applies
    to the whole result.

    Raises ``ValueError`` on empty input,
    unknown units, or a trailing unitless part.
    """
    if not value:
        raise ValueError(
            "Duration must not be empty"
        )
    sign, body = 1, value
    if body[0] in "+-":
        sign = -1 if body[0] == "-" else 1
        body = body[1:]
        if not body:
            raise ValueError(
                f"Invalid duration: {value!r}"
            )
    try:
        return sign * float(body)
    except ValueError:
        pass
    tokens = _DURATION_TOKEN.findall(body)
    if not tokens or "".join(
        n + s for n, s in tokens
    ) != body:
        raise ValueError(
            f"Invalid duration: {value!r}"
        )
    total = 0.0
    for num, suf in tokens:
        if not suf or suf not in suffixes:
            raise ValueError(
                f"Unknown or missing unit "
                f"in {value!r}"
            )
        total += float(num) * suffixes[suf]
    return sign * total
@

The [[--timeout]] option feeds into [[subprocess]] which wants seconds,
so a bare number defaults to seconds:

<<helper functions>>=
def parse_timeout(value):
    """Parse a duration string to seconds.

    Bare number = seconds.  Suffixes: ``s``, ``m``,
    ``h``, ``d``.
    """
