\subsubsection{Per-Window Active Labels}
\label{track:window-auto-suspend}

Window auto-suspend has different semantics from pane auto-suspend.
Instead of following terminal focus, it follows whether the tracked tmux
window is selected by any attached client.  To support that, we keep a
window-scoped labels file analogous to the pane labels file.  As with
pane state, the filename includes the tmux server key so two different
tmux server instances sharing a filesystem do not reuse the same
window-state path for identical [[@...]] identifiers.  The instance
distinction matters when a server is killed and restarted without a
reboot: the new server reuses the same socket path \emph{and}
restarts window numbering at [[@0]], so only the generation part of
the key keeps the dead server's files from being mistaken for the
new server's windows (\cref{track:server-key}).  A reboot instead
clears the runtime state directory itself
(\cref{track:tmux-state-dir}).

<<helper functions>>=
def window_labels_path(
    window_id: str,
    who: str,
    tmux_server_key: str,
) -> pathlib.Path:
    """Return path to a tracked window's labels file."""
    safe_window = window_id.replace("@", "window_")
    return (
        get_tmux_state_dir()
        / f"window_labels_{sanitize_tmux_id(tmux_server_key)}_{safe_window}"
        f"_{sanitize_who(who)}.json"
    )
@

Tmux's [[session-window-changed]] hook runs the switch command via
[[run-shell]], which spawns an asynchronous subprocess.  By the time
that subprocess queries tmux for the \enquote{last window} (via
[[$0:!]]), the pointer has already advanced---empirically, both
[[#{window_id}]] and [[$0:!]] resolve to the same window after the
switch.  We therefore maintain our own state file that records the
last selected window per session and worker.  Each invocation of
[[window_switch_detached]] reads the previous value (the source
window) and writes the current value, giving the next invocation
a reliable baseline.

<<helper functions>>=
def last_selected_window_path(
    who: str,
    tmux_server_key: str,
    session_id: str,
) -> pathlib.Path:
    """Return path to the last-selected-window state file."""
    return (
        get_tmux_state_dir()
        / f"last_window"
        f"_{sanitize_tmux_id(tmux_server_key)}"
        f"_{sanitize_tmux_id(session_id)}"
        f"_{sanitize_who(who)}.txt"
    )


def read_last_selected_window(
    who: str,
    tmux_server_key: str,
    session_id: str,
) -> str | None:
    """Read the last-selected window ID, or ``None``."""
    path = last_selected_window_path(
        who, tmux_server_key, session_id,
    )
    try:
        text = path.read_text().strip()
        return text if text else None
    except OSError:
        return None


def write_last_selected_window(
    who: str,
    tmux_server_key: str,
    session_id: str,
    window_id: str,
) -> None:
    """Record the currently selected window ID."""
    path = last_selected_window_path(
        who, tmux_server_key, session_id,
    )
    tmp = path.with_suffix(".tmp")
    tmp.write_text(window_id + "\n")
    tmp.rename(path)
@

We reuse the same absent-versus-empty distinction as for panes:
[[None]] means the window is not tracked, while an empty visible label
set means the tracked file exists but currently has no labels.

Preserving that distinction is what keeps a window tracked across a full
stop.  When the user stops every label in a still-open window the count
dict drains to empty, but the window has not gone away: the file must
survive as an empty marker so a later resume re-attaches its labels to
the window instead of starting them as global, window-less labels.  We
therefore return the empty dict verbatim and reserve [[None]] for a
genuinely absent file.

Unlike pane state, window state may receive overlapping contributions.
Two panes in the same tracked window can both contribute [[work]], and a
single pane exit must not delete [[work]] globally while the other pane
still owns it.  We therefore persist per-label reference counts on disk.
The public read helper still returns the visible ordered union of labels,
and it accepts the older flat-list format for compatibility by treating
each listed label as count~1.

<<helper functions>>=
def read_window_label_counts(
    window_id: str,
    who: str,
    tmux_server_key: str,
) -> dict[str, int] | None:
    """Read tracked window label counts, or ``None`` if absent."""
    path = window_labels_path(
        window_id, who, tmux_server_key,
    )
    try:
        data = json.loads(path.read_text())
    except (OSError, json.JSONDecodeError):
        return None

    if isinstance(data, list):
        counts = {}
        for label in data:
            if label not in counts:
                counts[label] = 1
        return counts

    if isinstance(data, dict):
        counts = {}
        for label, count in data.items():
            if isinstance(count, int) and count > 0:
                counts[label] = count
        return counts

    return None

<<helper functions>>=
def read_window_labels(
    window_id: str,
    who: str,
    tmux_server_key: str,
) -> list[str] | None:
    """Read tracked window labels, or ``None`` if absent."""
    counts = read_window_label_counts(
        window_id, who, tmux_server_key,
    )
    if counts is None:
        return None
    return list(counts)
@

Writing again uses an atomic temporary file in the same directory.

<<helper functions>>=
def write_window_label_counts(
    window_id: str,
    who: str,
    tmux_server_key: str,
    label_counts: dict[str, int],
) -> None:
    """Atomically write a tracked window's label counts."""
    path = window_labels_path(
        window_id, who, tmux_server_key,
    )
    path.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    tmp = path.with_suffix(".tmp")
    tmp.write_text(json.dumps(label_counts))
    tmp.rename(path)


def write_window_labels(
    window_id: str,
    who: str,
    tmux_server_key: str,
    labels: list[str],
) -> None:
    """Atomically write a tracked window's labels."""
    write_window_label_counts(
        window_id,
        who,
        tmux_server_key,
        {
            label: 1 for label in dict.fromkeys(labels)
        },
    )
@

As with panes, we merge new labels while preserving the visible order.
Repeated contributions increment the label's reference count instead of
duplicating it in the visible list.

<<helper functions>>=
def add_window_labels(
    window_id: str,
    who: str,
    tmux_server_key: str,
    labels: list[str],
) -> None:
    """Add labels to a tracked window's active set."""
    existing = read_window_label_counts(
        window_id, who, tmux_server_key,
    ) or {}
    for lbl in labels:
        if lbl not in existing:
            existing[lbl] = 0
        existing[lbl] += 1
    write_window_label_counts(
        window_id, who, tmux_server_key, existing,
    )
@

Removing labels decrements the corresponding reference counts and only
drops a visible label once the last contributing pane has gone away.
Draining the dict to empty is deliberately \emph{not} a deletion: an
empty count dict means \enquote{no labels are active right now}, which is
not the same fact as \enquote{this window is gone}.  A user can stop
every label in a window that is still open and expect to resume them
there later, so we always write the (possibly empty) dict back and leave
the file in place as the window's tracked marker.  Genuine teardown ---
deleting the file once the window itself disappears --- lives in the
pane-exit cleanup command, which can ask tmux whether the window still
exists (see \cref{track:detached-finalize}).

<<helper functions>>=
def remove_window_labels(
    window_id: str,
    who: str,
    tmux_server_key: str,
    labels: list[str],
) -> None:
    """Remove labels from a tracked window's active set."""
    existing = read_window_label_counts(
        window_id, who, tmux_server_key,
    )
    if existing is None:
        return
    for label in dict.fromkeys(labels):
        count = existing.get(label)
        if count is None:
            continue
        if count <= 1:
            del existing[label]
        else:
            existing[label] = count - 1
    write_window_label_counts(
        window_id, who, tmux_server_key, existing,
    )
@

Because [[remove_window_labels]] no longer deletes, the only caller that
removes a window file outright is the pane-exit cleanup command, once it
has confirmed the window itself is gone.  We keep that unlink in its own
idempotent helper so the cleanup path can call it unconditionally without
caring whether the file was already absent.

<<helper functions>>=
def delete_window_labels(
    window_id: str,
    who: str,
    tmux_server_key: str,
) -> None:
    """Remove a tracked window's labels file."""
    try:
        window_labels_path(
            window_id, who, tmux_server_key,
        ).unlink()
    except OSError:
        pass
