\subsubsection{Installing Focus Hooks}

Unlike the exit hooks, which use a pane-existence check (see
[[install_tmux_exit_hooks]]), focus hooks use tmux's format-string
pane-ID comparison ([[#{==:#{pane_id},...}]]).  The distinction
matters: exit hooks fire \emph{after} a pane dies, so we must ask
whether the tracked pane still exists; focus hooks fire while the
tracked pane is alive, so [[#{pane_id}]] correctly resolves to the
pane that gained or lost focus and we can compare it directly.

<<helper functions>>=
def install_tmux_focus_hooks(
    pane_id: str,
    who: str,
    tmux_server_key: str,
) -> None:
    """Install pane-focus-out and pane-focus-in hooks.

    The hooks use a format-string pane-ID guard (not the
    pane-existence check used by exit hooks) because
    focus events fire while the tracked pane is alive.
    """
    suspend_cmd = build_detached_suspend_command(
        who, pane_id, tmux_server_key,
    )
    resume_cmd = build_detached_resume_command(
        who, pane_id, tmux_server_key,
    )
    for hook_name, shell_cmd in (
        ("pane-focus-out", suspend_cmd),
        ("pane-focus-in", resume_cmd),
    ):
        guarded = (
            "if-shell -F "
            f"'#{{==:#{{pane_id}},{pane_id}}}' "
            f"{{ run-shell -b {shlex.quote(shell_cmd)} }}"
        )
        subprocess.run(
            [
                "tmux", "set-hook", "-p",
                "-t", pane_id,
                hook_name, guarded,
            ],
            check=True,
        )
@

Window launches intentionally do \emph{not} use
[[pane-focus-out]]/[[pane-focus-in]] to infer window transitions.  Those
pane hooks fire whenever the terminal emulator loses focus---for example
when the user Alt-Tabs to a browser or PDF reader---which is too
aggressive for window-scoped tracking.

Instead, the authoritative state is [[last_selected_window]]: one file
per worker, tmux server, and tmux session recording which window nytid
believes tmux has selected.  The detached
[[session-window-changed]] command compares that file with tmux's live
selected window, snapshots the source and target labels under
[[with_tmux_context_lock]], writes the new authoritative value, and then
executes the stop/start transition from that captured state.

This invariant filters out terminal blur directly.  Alt-Tab away from
the terminal does not change tmux's selected window, so no
[[session-window-changed]] event fires and no transition runs.  Only a
real tmux window selection change can stop or start tracked labels.

Tracked-window launch uses the same invariant.  [[run_tmux_command]]
holds [[with_tmux_context_lock]] only across [[tmux new-window]], label
publication, and the [[last_selected_window]] write.  After the lock is
released, it performs the stop/start transition inline and then installs
the authoritative session hook.  Any detached hook spawned by
[[new-window]] runs via [[run-shell -b]] after that state is already
published, where the [[prev == selected]] guard discards the redundant
event.

Because multiple nytid launches may target the same tmux session, we
must avoid appending duplicate hook entries.  The
[[install_tmux_hook_once]] helper queries existing hooks first and skips
the append when the exact command string is already registered.

Exact-string deduplication alone has a failure mode, though: the hook
command bakes in the [[--server-key]], so whenever the key computation
changes---an upgrade, or the [[$TMUX]]-PID instability of
\cref{track:server-key}---the new command no longer matches the old
line and gets \emph{appended} next to it.  The old hooks keep firing
forever, rewriting state files under keys nothing reads anymore.
Observed in the wild: dozens of accumulated hooks carrying three
different key formats on one session.  The installer therefore takes
an optional [[is_stale]] predicate identifying hook lines that this
command \emph{supersedes}; matching lines are unset by their array
index before the append.  Unsetting one index of a tmux array option
leaves the other indices in place, so unrelated hooks (and other
workers' hooks, which the predicate must not match) survive.

<<helper functions>>=
def install_tmux_hook_once(
    target: str,
    hook_name: str,
    command: str,
    is_stale=None,
) -> None:
    """Append a tmux hook command once without clobbering others.

    Existing hook lines that `is_stale` classifies as superseded
    (and that differ from `command`) are unset first.
    """
    show_result = subprocess.run(
        ["tmux", "show-hooks", "-t", target],
        capture_output=True,
        text=True,
        check=False,
    )
    existing_lines = [
        line for line in show_result.stdout.splitlines()
        if line.startswith(f"{hook_name}[")
        or line.startswith(f"{hook_name} ")
    ]
    <<unset superseded hook lines>>
    if any(command in line for line in existing_lines):
        return
    subprocess.run(
        [
            "tmux", "set-hook", "-a",
            "-t", target,
            hook_name,
            command,
        ],
        check=True,
    )
