\subsubsection{Adopting an Untracked Window}
\label{track:adopt}

Window tracking so far only begins at [[track start --window]],
which creates the window itself.  That leaves no way to track a
window that already exists: one created with tmux's own
[[new-window]], or one that lost its state file (the historical
sweeping bug above, or temporary-file aging,
\cref{track:tmux-state-dir}).  The [[adopt]] command fills that
gap: it is [[start --window]] minus the launch, run from inside
the window to adopt.

Adoption both marks the window tracked \emph{and} starts its
labels.  The two must not be separable: the user demonstrably sits
in this window (they typed the command here), and a
tracked-but-idle selected window is exactly the inconsistent state
the window machinery works to prevent---the next window switch
away would try to stop labels that never ran.  For the same
consistency reason, adoption mirrors a launch's context switch:
when auto-suspend is on and the previously selected window was
tracked, that window's labels stop as ours start.

We resolve the server key with [[compute_tmux_server_key]] rather
than [[get_current_tmux_server_key]], for the same reason
[[clean]] does (\cref{track:server-sweep}): the latter's no-state
guard returns [[None]] precisely when no windows are tracked
yet---the very situation adopt exists to fix.  The window and
session ids come from the live pane in one query; an inherited
[[TMUX_WINDOW_ID]] could be stale, the pane's actual position
cannot.

<<helper functions>>=
def get_tmux_pane_context(
    pane_id: str,
) -> tuple[str, str] | None:
    """Return (window id, session id) of ``pane_id``, or ``None``."""
    try:
        result = subprocess.run(
            [
                "tmux",
                "display-message",
                "-p",
                "-t",
                pane_id,
                "#{window_id} #{session_id}",
            ],
            capture_output=True,
            text=True,
            check=True,
        )
    except (subprocess.CalledProcessError, OSError):
        return None
    parts = result.stdout.split()
    if len(parts) != 2:
        return None
    return parts[0], parts[1]
@

